## 7. International Travel

### 7.1 Visa and Passport

**Company Responsibility:**
- Company covers visa application fees for business travel
- Visa cost is reimbursed (submit original receipt and visa sticker in passport)

**Employee Responsibility:**
- Passport must be valid for at least 6 months beyond travel date
- Employee is responsible for tracking passport validity and applying for renewal
- Any delays in passport renewal causing travel cancellation are not compensated

### 7.2 Travel Insurance

**Mandatory for:**
- International travel >5 days
- Travel to countries with higher health/safety risks (defined by company, typically Southeast Asia, Africa, Middle East beyond GCC)

**Company Covers:**
- Premium for standard travel insurance (typically USD 50–100)
- Medical emergency coverage up to USD 100,000
- Travel delay coverage (usually included in policy)

**Employee Responsibility:**
- Purchase insurance within 7 days of travel confirmation
- Provide insurance policy document and proof of payment
- Claim handling: If incident occurs, employee can claim directly from insurance; company reimburses or provides advance

### 7.3 Currency and Foreign Exchange

**Advance Currency:**
- For trips >7 days or to countries with limited ATM access, the employee can request INR-to-foreign currency advance
- Advance is processed by Finance team; typically takes 3–5 working days
- Exchange rate used: Current OANDA rate + 1% mark-up

**Expense Reporting:**
- All expenses in foreign currency are reported in the original currency
- System auto-converts to INR using the daily exchange rate on expense date
- Employees do not need to adjust for exchange rate fluctuations

**GST on International Expenses:**
- For India-based employees, GST is applicable on airline tickets (5–12% depending on class); this is included in the flight cost reimbursement
- For international hotel accommodations, local taxes are reimbursed as part of the hotel bill

### 7.4 International Business Etiquette

Employees traveling internationally should:
- Carry business cards and company letterhead documents
- Be aware of local business customs and dress codes
- Maintain professional email and phone communication
- Report any security concerns to the manager and HR immediately
- Keep the manager/HR updated on travel status (especially for high-risk regions)

